{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And he said to his people, Behold, etc.</b> His saying ״הִנֵּה״ etc. is to be explained along the lines of what Amram, of blessed memory, stated (Genesis Rabbah 50:12) on the verse (Genesis 34:30) ״עֲכַרְתֶּם אוֹתִי״ — that the cask was thoroughly sunken, etc., for there was a tradition in hand, etc., that they were destined to fall into the hand of Israel — end quote. Therefore the king of Egypt said ״הִנֵּה עַם בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — known for strength and might; behold, they are numerous and powerful." "And he said <b>Come, let us deal wisely with him, lest he multiply</b> etc. <b>and go up from the land</b>, and do what is handed over in their power — namely, that he will destroy every living soul of the Canaanite, his brother. Therefore they must scheme against him to imprison him so he cannot leave. And for this reason Egypt is called a prison-house, for the Egyptians guarded Israel from leaving there, out of fear lest he destroy his brother the Canaanite, etc." "<b>Further,</b> it intends by saying <b>behold, a people</b> etc. along this line: this people is different from all other peoples, for the other peoples intermingle among themselves, and there is no people that does not contain great admixtures from many peoples — but not so the people of Israel. And that is what he means by <b>behold, a people</b> — entirely the children of Israel, with no stranger among them. And his saying <b>numerous and powerful</b> means that insofar as they are one unified people, even though they may be few compared to the other nations, they count as more numerous and more powerful than they, because each one of them gives his life for his brother — and this is well known to those who understand the nature of warfare." "<b>Further,</b> it intends by saying <b>numerous and powerful</b>: ״numerous״ — that they multiply contrary to the way of the world. Now it is the nature of the world that when many are born, they will be weakened in strength, both in respect of those born and in respect of those giving birth — go out and learn from twins, as is known to the natural philosophers. From this it follows that one need not fear their multitude, for in the end they will diminish; and even if they are many, they have no strength that one need fear their proliferation. Therefore he said ״and powerful״ — meaning, even though they multiply more than usual, nonetheless ״and more powerful than we are.״ Even if they do not have the multitude, it would be fitting for them to be considered stronger than they. And from this it necessarily follows that one must scheme against them. Accordingly, the word ״מִמֶּנּוּ״ (than we are) refers back to ״עָצוּם״ (powerful) and not to his saying ״רַב״ (numerous), for immediately afterward he says ״פֶּן יִרְבֶּה״ (lest he multiply)." "<b>Further,</b> it intends by saying <b>numerous and powerful more than we are</b>, meaning that their greatness and their proliferation came from us. And the intention in this is that he comes to inform them that they have an obligation owed by Israel, and he intends to set forth a claim in advance, so that his people — or anyone who sees his deeds as foreign — would not say to him: by what authority do you stand and take hold of one people dwelling in your midst and enslave them with slave-labor? Therefore he said <b>behold, the people of the children of Israel</b> — this itself, his own flesh and blood, is from us, for we kept them alive during the years of famine, and all that has multiplied from them is ours as well, for they came to sojourn in the land with seventy souls. And from now on he will hold Israel with a strong hand together with a claim, and they will scheme against him to do with him as seems good in their eyes." "<b>Further</b>, it intends that you will find that all the kings and great ones of the nations scheme to glorify their kingdom through a prestigious people — how much more so with the people Israel, for a name and distinction uniquely belongs to it. And you will find no nation that ruled over Israel that was not superior to all the other nations, in the sod of (Lamentations 1:5) ״הָיוּ צָרֶיהָ לְרֹאשׁ״. Therefore the new king said: ״הִנֵּה בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — the prestigious ones, they are the essence of the kingdom more than the Egyptians, and through them it is held in esteem. If they leave the city, the city will become diminished and contemptible. Therefore <b>come, let us deal wisely with him</b>, etc. — lest he leave the city by means of some pretext when war is proclaimed, and he recoils from the war and goes his way, and then we cannot stop him, for he will wage war against us together with the enemy fighting against us."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:23:20.320388758Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}